Explanation:
Step-by-Step Solution:
1. Statutory Text & Synergy: Section 17, Republic Act No. 10121 & Republic Act No. 7581 (Price Act):
 'Remedial Measures. — The declaration of a state of calamity shall make mandatory the immediate undertaking of the following remedial measures:
 (a) Mandatory imposition of PRICE CEILING ON BASIC NECESSITIES AND PRIME COMMODITIES by the DTI, DA, and DOH pursuant to RA 7581;
 (b) Monitoring, prevention and control of overpricing/profiteering and hoarding of prime commodities, medicines and petroleum products;
 (c) PROGRAMMED DISBURSEMENT OF THE QUICK RESPONSE FUND (QRF) for relief, rescue, and rehabilitation;
 (d) GRANT OF NO-INTEREST LOANS by government financial institutions (GSIS, SSS, Pag-IBIG, LandBank, DBP) to the most affected populations...'
2. Option A matches the statutory effects verbatim.

Common Trap: Believing prices can be increased during a calamity; declaring a State of Calamity TRIGGERS AN AUTOMATIC STATUTORY PRICE FREEZE on basic food, water, and medicines.
Exam Tip: Legal Effects of State of Calamity = AUTOMATIC PRICE FREEZE (RA 7581) + Release of Quick Response Fund (QRF) + Zero-interest Calamity Loans.

Step-by-Step Solution:
1. Statutory Text: Section 19, Republic Act No. 10121 (Prohibited Acts):
 'Prohibited Acts. — Any person, group or corporation who commits any of the following prohibited acts shall be held liable and be subjected to the penalties provided in Section 20 of this Act:
 (a) Dereliction of duties which leads to destruction, loss of lives, critical damage of facilities;
 (b) PREVENTING THE ENTRY AND DISTRIBUTION OF RELIEF GOODS IN DISASTER-STRICKEN AREAS, including appropriate technology, tools, equipment, accessories, disasterteams/experts;
 (c) BUYING, FOR CONSUMPTION OR RESALE, FROM PERSONS WHO RECEIVED DISASTER RELIEF PACKS;
 (d) BUYING, FOR CONSUMPTION OR RESALE, DISASTER RELIEF GOODS, EQUIPMENT OR OTHER AID COMMODITIES WHICH ARE INTENDED FOR DISTRIBUTION TO DISASTER VICTIMS;
 (e) CONVERTING, SELLING OR REDISTRIBUTING RELIEF GOODS;
 (f) TAMPERING WITH OR SUBSTITUTING RELIEF GOODS, equipment or other aid commodities with other goods or commodities;
 (g) DELIBERATELY SPREADING FALSE OR ALARMING INFORMATION BY ANY MEANS OR MEDIA...'
2. Penalties (Section 20): Fines of PHP 50,000 to PHP 500,000 and/or imprisonment of 6 to 12 years, plus perpetual disqualification from public office for public officials.
3. Option A is the verbatim statutory enumeration of prohibited disaster crimes.

Common Trap: Thinking fake news during typhoons is just a social media post; DELIBERATELY SPREADING FALSE DISASTER INFORMATION OR HOARDING RELIEF GOODS IS A MAJOR CRIME under Section 19 of RA 10121.
Exam Tip: Prohibited Acts during Disasters (RA 10121, Sec. 19) = Stealing/reselling relief goods + Tampering relief packs + Blocking relief distribution + SPREADING FALSE DISASTER INFORMATION.
